// XFAIL: RT-479: UNSOUND: a `requires` that calls `go(p)`, which writes the caller's memory through `let s = unsafe { &mut (*p).0[..] }; s[0] = 9;` (p: `*mut ([i64; 2], i64)`), is accepted: the program prints 1, then `9 2`, the caller's tuple changed by a condition; contract.effect refuses it (0.30.2)
// From the third re-review of g302holes. Since v0.30.1 at least.
// The g302holes branch refuses the same clause through `&mut (*p).0` or
// `&mut (*p)[..]` of a `*mut [i64; 2]` ("calls 'go', which writes
// through a raw pointer what it did not make"); a tuple field under an
// Index is not seen as a lend of the pointee. The release build accepts
// all three. The callee-returning form is rt_479b.
// ERROR: contract.effect
fn go(p: *mut ([i64; 2], i64)) -> bool {
    // UNSAFE: test
    // SAFETY: test
    let s = unsafe { &mut (*p).0[..] };
    s[0] = 9;
    true
}

fn f(p: *mut ([i64; 2], i64)) -> i64
    requires go(p)
{
    1
}

fn main() {
    let mut t = ([1i64, 2], 3i64);
    println("{}", f(&raw mut t));
    println("{} {}", t.0[0], t.0[1]);
}
